    <body>
      <sec id="article-133971.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Failed or difficult tracheal intubation remains a critical challenge in airway management and a significant contributor to preventable morbidity and mortality in anesthesia, emergency medicine, pulmonary, and critical care settings. This course details evidence from large audits and closed-claims analyses demonstrating that many adverse outcomes arise from inadequate airway assessment, lack of contingency planning, repeated unsuccessful intubation attempts, and delayed recognition of esophageal intubation.</p>
        <p>This activity reviews lessons learned from failed airway cases and highlights current evidence-based strategies for managing both anticipated and unanticipated difficult airways. Comprehensive airway evaluation, recognition of anatomic and physiologic predictors of difficulty, appropriate use of video laryngoscopy and fiberoptic techniques, and adherence to consensus guidelines for airway safety are also discussed. Participants will gain&#x000a0;an understanding of the decision-making involved, the preparation needed for airway emergencies, and structured management strategies that reduce complications and improve patient outcomes.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying predictors of failure related to video laryngoscopy, supraglottic airway placement, and front-of-neck access rescue, performing the recommended techniques for difficult tracheal intubation, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing intubations in individuals with difficult airways.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify predictors associated with difficult tracheal intubation during preprocedural airway assessment.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based airway management guidelines to select appropriate tracheal intubation techniques.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine appropriate backup airway strategies when initial intubation attempts fail in order to prevent life-threatening complications.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with interprofessional healthcare teams to coordinate management strategies of difficult airways to improve safety and inform future airway management.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=133971&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=133971">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133971.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>For anesthesiologists, emergency medicine physicians, pulmonary specialists, and critical care physicians, tracheal intubation is a foundational, life-saving procedural skill. Effective preparation requires a review of the patient&#x02019;s history and presenting pathology, a comprehensive airway examination, and the assembly of the necessary equipment. Airway management planning requires a clearly defined primary strategy along with backup approaches in the event of failure. Poor clinical judgment and inadequate preparation may lead to adverse outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Certain patients present with difficult intubations that cannot be predicted during preprocedural evaluation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Therefore, understanding the lessons derived from failed airway cases provides clinicians with knowledge and strategies to manage both anticipated and, more importantly, unanticipated difficult or failed intubations.</p>
        <p>The Fourth National Audit Project (NAP4) of the Royal College of Anaesthetists and the Difficult Airway Society (DAS) executive summary (2011) reported several contributing factors associated with airway management complications, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Failure to assess the airway</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Failure to have a plan B or plan C when the initial plan A fails</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Failure to proceed with awake fiberoptic intubation even when indicated</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Repeated attempts at intubation despite deteriorating oxygenation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>History of previous head and neck cancer resection or radiation-induced deformities</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Obesity as a risk factor for airway management difficulty</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Failure of emergency percutaneous cricothyrotomy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Aspiration with repeated intubation attempts resulting in death</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Failure to recognize an esophageal intubation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r2">[2]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Severe complications, including death, hypoxic brain injury, or emergency surgical airway, occurred at a rate of 1 in 22,000 within a database of 2.9 million general anesthetics. Complication rates increased markedly in emergency departments and intensive care units, where the incidence reached 35 and 55 times higher, respectively. Underreporting of critical events likely resulted in a true incidence exceeding reported estimates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>The Seventh National Audit Project (NAP7), conducted from June 2021 through June 2022, examined perioperative cardiac arrest. Major hemorrhage represented the most common cause of intraoperative cardiac arrest; however, failed airway management and unrecognized esophageal intubation also contributed to these events.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>The Project for Universal Management of Airways and multiple international airway societies have developed consensus guidelines intended to classify unrecognized esophageal intubation as a &#x0201c;never event.&#x0201d; Key recommendations include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Exhaled CO<sub>2</sub>&#x000a0;monitoring and pulse oximetry should always be used during airway management.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use of video laryngoscopy routinely, as this allows others to share the view and confirm that the endotracheal tube is in the trachea.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Absence of sustained exhaled CO<sub>2</sub>, defined as at least 7 breaths with an amplitude greater than 7.5 mm Hg, requires immediate exclusion of esophageal intubation. Appropriate responses may include removal of the tube, return to mask ventilation, or placement of a supraglottic airway, or repeat laryngoscopy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Clinical examination alone, including observation of chest rise or auscultation of breath sounds, should not serve as the primary indicator of correct tube placement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r7">[7]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Discouraging findings also emerged from the American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) closed-claims analysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r3">[3]</xref> Some adverse outcomes occurred in otherwise healthy patients undergoing elective surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r8">[8]</xref> Such findings highlight numerous opportunities to strengthen airway management practices and improve patient safety and outcomes.</p>
        <p>Precise terminology remains essential when difficulty arises during airway management. Documentation should clarify whether the challenge involved difficult or failed mask ventilation, difficult or failed direct or video laryngoscopy, difficulty delivering the endotracheal tube during tracheal intubation, or difficult or failed supraglottic airway placement. Detailed documentation ensures that subsequent clinicians receive the information required to guide safe airway management for the same patient in future clinical encounters.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133971.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>Several anatomic features increase the likelihood of difficult tracheal intubation. Recognized predictors include small mouth opening, short thyromental distance, a full set of teeth with prominent incisors, reduced mandibular protrusion, reduced submandibular compliance, short neck, large neck circumference, limited neck extension, a Mallampati 3 or 4 oropharyngeal view, obesity, and surgical or radiation-induced anatomic changes. Each additional anatomic predictor further elevates the probability of difficult intubation. Patients with prior head and neck cancer resection&#x000a0;or radiation-induced deformities carry the highest risk for difficult or failed intubation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>The concept of the physiologically difficult airway represents a newer framework in airway management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r10">[10]</xref> This concept emphasizes underlying pathophysiologic conditions rather than anatomic abnormalities that increase the risk of airway management complications, including cardiac arrest occurring immediately after intubation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r11">[11]</xref> Optimization of physiologic status before airway intervention may reduce these complications.</p>
        <p>Clinical conditions associated with a physiologically difficult airway include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe hypoxia and respiratory failure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypotension and shock</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe metabolic acidosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Right ventricular failure&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>High aspiration risk</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Management strategies focus on physiologic stabilization before and during airway intervention. Recommended approaches include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Providing supplemental oxygen (low or high flow) or non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Treating hypotension with intravenous fluids or vasopressors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Delaying intubation until the severe metabolic acidosis is improved</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) to assess the severity of right ventricular dysfunction/failure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Choice of drugs based on the patient's hemodynamics</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Keeping the patient awake and breathing spontaneously&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r10">[10]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Situational or contextual factors may also complicate airway management and contribute to difficult or failed intubation. Contributing factors include clinician experience, clinical location, availability of assistance, access to appropriate equipment, and patient-related conditions, eg, intoxication or disability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133971.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>Several patient populations carry an elevated risk for difficult or failed tracheal intubation. These groups include pregnant patients requiring emergency general anesthesia, trauma patients with cervical spine injury with or without concurrent closed head injury, morbidly obese patients, and individuals with a history of head and neck cancer. A large multicenter study involving more than 14,000 general anesthetics in pregnant patients reported a risk of difficult intubation of 1 in 49 and a risk of failed intubation of 1 in 808.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r13">[13]</xref> Anatomical and physiological changes associated with pregnancy contribute to difficult intubation, rapid oxygen desaturation, and an increased risk of aspiration.</p>
        <p>The Obstetric Anesthetists Association&#x000a0;(OAA) and the&#x000a0;DAS developed guidelines for managing difficult or failed intubation in obstetric patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r15">[15]</xref> A central principle of obstetric airway management involves early placement of regional anesthesia in high-risk patients to reduce the likelihood of requiring tracheal intubation. Effective airway management in this setting also requires close collaboration with obstetrical and nursing teams.</p>
        <p>When general anesthesia becomes necessary, guideline recommendations emphasize clinical decision-making based on careful consideration of risks to both mother and fetus, as well as the urgency of delivery. Increasing support exists for the use of video laryngoscopy to improve first-pass intubation success. Following failed intubation, avoiding repeated attempts and promptly placing a second-generation supraglottic airway may represent the safest management strategy.</p>
        <p>Trauma patients with cervical spine injury warrant heightened suspicion for difficult intubation. Immobilization with a rigid cervical collar, higher Cormack-Lehane laryngeal views, and increased aspiration risk contribute to the complexity of airway management. Airway protection, mechanical ventilation, and preparation for emergency surgery frequently necessitate intubation in these patients. Concurrent closed head injury may also occur.</p>
        <p>Airway management in this population requires strict attention to minimizing cervical spine movement while preventing hypotension, hypoxia, and hypercarbia. Removal of the anterior portion of the rigid cervical collar may facilitate airway access. Limited evidence supports the effectiveness of cervical spine immobilization with a rigid collar, while its presence complicates bag-mask ventilation and laryngoscopy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Manual in-line stabilization and cricoid pressure remain widely accepted standards during endotracheal tube placement and should be maintained while minimizing neck movement. If these maneuvers obstruct airway access, temporary relaxation may facilitate successful intubation. Video laryngoscopy demonstrates higher first-pass success rates than direct laryngoscopy and represents a recommended technique in this clinical setting.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Obesity, particularly morbid obesity, introduces challenges throughout airway management, including bag-mask ventilation, laryngoscopy, and emergency cricothyrotomy. Two-thirds of patients with adverse outcomes reported in the NAP4 study had obesity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r2">[2]</xref> Reduced safe apneic time frequently leads to rapid oxygen desaturation in this population. Airway management strategies often require modification to address these physiologic limitations.</p>
        <p>Despite appropriate indications, anesthesiologists may avoid awake intubation because of production pressures, reduced confidence, or declining procedural proficiency. Skill maintenance has become increasingly difficult. A single tertiary care institution reported a declining incidence of awake tracheal intubations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r17">[17]</xref> Increased reliance on and success with video laryngoscopy likely contributed to this trend.&#x000a0;When airway anatomy is distorted by prior surgical resection, radiation-induced changes, or other pathological conditions, the flexible fiberoptic scope is the only instrument capable of the multi-axial maneuverability required for effective airway navigation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133971.s5" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>The following equipment should be readily available during intubation procedures in patients with risk factors for a difficult airway:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>A difficult airway cart fully stocked and readily available; all staff should know what is on the cart and where the cart is stored</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Video laryngoscopy, ideally, in every anesthetizing location</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Stock more than&#x000a0;1 type of supraglottic airway on the cart</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scalpel, bougie, and small endotracheal tube for emergency surgical airway</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cognitive aids, eg, the new ASA infographics and algorithms, the DAS guidelines, Canadian Airway Focus Group (CAFG) guidelines, or the Vortex algorithm, should be attached to the cart</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133971.s6" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>Anesthesiologists, emergency medicine clinicians, pulmonary clinicians, and critical care&#x000a0;clinicians are primarily&#x000a0;responsible for emergency airway management.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133971.s7" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>
<bold>Preparation</bold>
</p>
        <p>The following tools and&#x000a0;pearls may be utilized to help clinicians prepare for high-risk patients and for unanticipated difficult intubation.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Application of supplemental oxygen supports safe airway management during tracheal intubation. Placement of a low-flow nasal cannula for all patients, or high-flow oxygen when available, prolongs safe apnea time and proves particularly beneficial in patients with obesity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;The acronym NO DESAT, coined by Dr. Richard Levitan, reinforces the principle of continuous oxygen delivery during airway management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;This acronym stands for the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>N</bold>:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>nasal</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>O</bold>:&#x000a0;oxygen</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>D</bold>:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>during</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>E</bold>:&#x000a0;efforts</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>S</bold>:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>securing</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>A</bold>:&#x000a0;a</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>T</bold>:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>tube</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Anesthesiology Practice Guidelines</bold>
</p>
        <p>The most recent iteration of the American Society of Anesthesiologists Practice Guidelines for Management of the Difficult Airway was published in 2022 and introduced several significant updates compared with the 2012 version (see <bold>Image</bold>. ASA Difficult Airway Algorithm: Adult Patients).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r1">[1]</xref> These updates include revised algorithms that replace the 2012 guidelines and newly developed color-coded infographic cognitive aids designed to improve readability and usability during high-stress clinical situations. Methodological changes also occurred. In addition to an extensive literature review, guideline recommendations incorporated expert consensus opinion. Earlier versions of the guideline avoided issuing recommendations when evidence remained limited or inconclusive, often leaving clinicians without clear direction.</p>
        <p>International collaboration expanded during the development of the updated guidelines. The task force included 15 members representing the United States, India, Ireland, Italy, and Switzerland, as well as multiple subspecialty organizations. Numerous virtual meetings supported this collaboration during the COVID pandemic lockdowns. Additional innovations included preinduction decision-making infographics addressing both awake and anesthetized airway management.</p>
        <p>Challenges frequently arise after induction of anesthesia, and the infographic for airway management in anesthetized patients provides an effective visual cognitive aid. New recommendations also addressed pediatric patients, whereas previous editions of the ASA Practice Guideline focused exclusively on adults; guidance on pediatric patients remains outside the scope of this activity. The updated guidelines also emphasized oxygen delivery and confirmation of ventilation through CO<sub>2</sub> monitoring, while reinforcing the importance of maintaining situational awareness of airway attempts, elapsed time, and oxygen saturation. Furthermore, the expanded recommendations addressed safe extubation of the difficult airway.</p>
        <p>The&#x000a0;DAS published multiple airway management algorithms in 2015.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r21">[21]</xref> One algorithm of particular relevance focuses on the management of unanticipated difficult intubation in adult patients. Page 1 presents an overview of the algorithm, page 2 outlines strategies to optimize each attempt, and page 3 describes the technique for performing an open cricothyrotomy. The plans include the following recommendations:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Plan A</bold>:&#x000a0;This plan&#x000a0;prioritizes effective mask ventilation and successful tracheal intubation. When the initial attempt fails, recommended interventions include repositioning the patient&#x02019;s head or neck, administering a paralytic drug, applying external laryngeal manipulation, removing cricoid pressure, or using adjuncts, eg, a bougie. Failure of these measures prompts transition to Plan B.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Plan B</bold>:&#x000a0;This plan focuses on maintaining oxygenation using a supraglottic airway. Successful placement creates an opportunity to reassess the situation. Available options include aborting the procedure, continuing with the supraglottic airway in place, or using the device as a conduit for tracheal intubation. Persistent failure leads to Plan C.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Plan C</bold>: Plan C involves reattempting mask ventilation with paralysis and a 2-person technique. Continued failure requires escalation to Plan D.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Plan D</bold>:&#x000a0;This plan involves emergency front-of-neck access (eFONA) using an open scalpel cricothyrotomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r21">[21]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>This flowchart forms part of the DAS Guidelines for unanticipated difficult intubation in adults (2015) and should be used in conjunction with the text. The Current Difficult Airway Society guidelines have been in publication for 10 years. Updated guidelines will be released later this year at the World Airway Management Meeting (WAMM) in Florence, Italy. This event will bring together participants from the DAS of the United Kingdom and Ireland, the Society for Airway Management (SAM) from North America, and the European Airway Management Society (EAMS),&#x000a0;representing numerous European countries.</p>
        <p>The Canadian Airway Focus Group (CAFG) updated its airway management guidelines in 2021. Part 1 summarizes recommendations for managing difficult airways in unconscious patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r22">[22]</xref> Part 2 outlines recommendations for airway management in patients with anticipated difficult intubation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>The Vortex Cognitive Aid</bold>
</p>
        <p>The Vortex cognitive aid provides a simple and practical visual framework for airway management and was developed by Dr. Nicholas Chrimes in Australia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r24">[24]</xref> The model identifies 3 primary interventions capable of restoring oxygenation: effective mask ventilation, placement of a supraglottic airway, and endotracheal tube placement. Success with any of these interventions moves the clinician into the &#x0201c;green zone,&#x0201d; creating an opportunity to pause and reassess. Failure of all 3 interventions leads toward the center of the Vortex, representing a &#x0201c;cannot intubate and cannot oxygenate&#x0201d; (CICO) scenario that requires immediate establishment of an emergency surgical airway to restore oxygenation and return to the green zone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>The term &#x0201c;cannot intubate and cannot oxygenate&#x0201d; replaced the earlier phrase &#x0201c;cannot intubate and cannot ventilate.&#x0201d; Priority shifts toward preventing or reversing hypoxia through effective ventilation rather than focusing exclusively on intubation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Other Interventions</bold>
</p>
        <p>Several additional interventions may improve the success of airway management. Examples include repositioning the head or neck, applying external laryngeal manipulation (ELM), selecting a different laryngoscope blade type, or transitioning to video laryngoscopy. Adjuncts, eg, a bougie or stylet, may facilitate endotracheal tube placement. Suctioning secretions or blood may also improve visualization. Administration of a paralytic agent often enhances ventilation. This recommendation contrasts with traditional teaching that required demonstration of successful mask ventilation before administration of neuromuscular blockade.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>Guidelines emphasize a maximum of 3 plus 1 laryngoscopy attempts using direct and video techniques. Success after more than 3 attempts by the same clinician remains unlikely and increases the risk of airway trauma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r26">[26]</xref> Repeated attempts without altering the approach should be avoided. The additional &#x0201c;plus 1&#x0201d; attempt allows for a subsequent attempt by an experienced clinician arriving to assist. Notably, guidelines from the ASA, DAS, and CAFG all discourage repeated attempts at laryngoscopy because of the risk of complete airway loss.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>Use of a second-generation supraglottic airway device receives a strong recommendation. These devices separate respiratory and gastrointestinal pathways, contain cuffs capable of supporting higher sealing pressures, and provide greater protection against aspiration than earlier supraglottic airway designs.&#x000a0;Open cricothyrotomy, also known as eFONA, currently receives preference over percutaneous approaches. Findings from the NAP4 study demonstrated higher rescue success rates and fewer complications with the open technique compared with needle cricothyroidotomy when emergency surgical airway access became necessary.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Additional guidelines address airway management in intensive care units (ICU), awake tracheal intubation, and extubation strategies. A comprehensive review of these guidelines can further support safe and effective airway management practices.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133971.s8" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>Video laryngoscopy has transformed airway management, with adoption in routine practice accelerating during the COVID-19 pandemic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r27">[27]</xref> This technique allows clinicians to &#x0201c;look around the corner&#x0201d; rather than aligning the oral, pharyngeal, and laryngeal axes, which form the basis of direct laryngoscopy.</p>
        <p>Cormack-Lehane laryngeal views often improve by one to two grades with video laryngoscopy, resulting in higher first-pass success. Many experts now recommend video laryngoscopy as the first-choice technique, and several society guidelines include this recommendation. A Cochrane Review analyzing 222 studies (219 RCTs and 3 quasi-RCTs) with over 26,000 patients concluded that video laryngoscopy consistently yields higher first-pass success and lower rates of failed intubation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r28">[28]</xref> A recent cluster-randomized trial of over 8,000 surgical patients reported that 1.7% required more than one intubation attempt with a hyperangulated video laryngoscope, compared with 7.6% with direct laryngoscopy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r29">[29]</xref> Similar improvements were observed in critically ill patients in emergency departments and intensive care units.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Video laryngoscopes fall into three main categories: Macintosh-style blades, hyperangulated blades, and channeled devices. The Macintosh-style blade has a distally positioned camera compared with direct laryngoscopy, allowing the &#x0201c;look around the corner&#x0201d; technique and facilitating most intubations. Hyperangulated blades perform best in anticipated difficult airways,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r32">[32]</xref> but using them in patients without difficult airway predictors may complicate tube delivery. Channeled video laryngoscopes feature an acute-angle blade with a guiding conduit for the endotracheal tube, minimizing neck movement and proving especially useful in patients with unstable cervical spine injuries. The term video laryngoscopy is often used generically, so careful attention to the specific blade type under discussion is essential when reviewing literature.</p>
        <p>Visualization of the glottic opening rarely limits success during video laryngoscopy. More common challenges occur during tube delivery, which can be mitigated by positioning the patient&#x02019;s head in a neutral position rather than the &#x0201c;sniffing&#x0201d; position. Obtaining a deliberate &#x0201c;limited&#x0201d; glottic view can also improve the trajectory for endotracheal tube placement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>Supraglottic airways (SGAs) serve as conduits for successful intubation in various clinical scenarios. Modified endotracheal tubes can be passed through an intubating laryngeal mask airway under fiberoptic guidance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r34">[34]</xref> Blind passage is no longer recommended; the tube should be loaded on a flexible fiberoptic scope for accurate guidance into the trachea. Use of this device by anesthesiologists has declined, likely due to familiarity with alternative SGAs, prior successful use, or unavailability of the intubating laryngeal mask. However, emergency medicine physicians have shown renewed interest with high success rates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>The intubating laryngeal mask airway is particularly effective in cases of lingual tonsillar hyperplasia or hypertrophy, a cause of unanticipated difficult or failed intubation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r36">[36]</xref> This reactive lymphoid tissue lies below the usual examination field and can obstruct endotracheal tube placement. The device displaces this tissue, creating space for successful tube passage.&#x000a0;Documentation of airway findings in the patient&#x02019;s chart is essential for future procedures. Anatomic conditions may change over time due to factors, eg, weight fluctuations or recent upper respiratory tract infections.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Other SGAs can also function as intubation conduits by loading an endotracheal tube or airway exchange catheter over a flexible fiberoptic scope and advancing it through the SGA into the trachea. Most SGAs can be adapted for this purpose, although minor modifications may be required depending on the device. This approach underlies the inclusion of SGAs as conduits in the&#x000a0;DAS guidelines for unanticipated difficult intubation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133971.s9" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of difficult or failed airway management include hypoxia, aspiration, esophageal intubation, emergency surgical airway, cardiovascular instability, cardiac arrhythmias, ischemic encephalopathy, and death.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133971.s10" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Unanticipated difficult intubations continue to occur despite advances in airway management. Clinicians must maintain comprehensive knowledge of predictors of failure for each device. When oxygen saturation declines and fine motor skills deteriorate, only four options remain: effective mask ventilation, placement of a supraglottic airway, placement of an endotracheal tube, or emergency surgical airway.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Predictive Mnemonics</bold>
</p>
        <p>Predictors of difficult or failed video laryngoscopy can be remembered with the following mnemonic CRANE:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>C</bold>:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Contamination due to blood, secretions, or vomit, or high Cormack Lehane view with direct laryngoscopy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>R</bold>:&#x000a0;Radiation&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>A</bold>:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Abnormal anatomy, including mass, previous surgery, or limited mouth opening</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>N</bold>:&#x000a0;Neck (is thick or has a flexion deformity)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>E</bold>:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Epiglottis or enlarged tongue</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Clinician inexperience with video laryngoscopy may further increase the risk of failure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>Predictors of difficult or failed supraglottic airway placement can be recalled using&#x000a0;the following mnemonic RODS:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>R</bold>:&#x000a0;Restricted mouth opening</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>O</bold>:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Obstruction in the upper airway</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>D</bold>:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Disrupted or distorted airway that prevents the device from &#x0201c;seating&#x0201d;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>S</bold>:<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Stiff lungs or stiff cervical spine</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Cricoid pressure or obesity may also complicate SGA placement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>Predictors of difficult or failed eFONA are summarized by&#x000a0;the following mnemonic SHORT:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>S</bold>:&#x000a0;Surgery with resultant neck distortion</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>H</bold>:&#x000a0;Hematoma or infection at the site</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>O</bold>:&#x000a0;Obese&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>R</bold>:&#x000a0;Radiation&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>T</bold>:&#x000a0;Tumor&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Female sex may additionally contribute to difficulty.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients with head and neck cancer&#x02013;related surgical or radiation deformities face increased risk of rescue failure across all airway devices, necessitating consideration of awake techniques and flexible fiberoptic intubation. Obesity, particularly morbid obesity, can further complicate airway management and may indicate awake intubation as the safest approach in select cases.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-133971.s11" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Tracheal intubation remains a foundational, life-saving skill for anesthesiologists, emergency medicine, pulmonary, and critical care physicians, yet unanticipated difficult or failed intubations continue to contribute to serious complications, including hypoxia, aspiration, esophageal intubation, cardiovascular instability, and death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r37">[37]</xref> Both anatomic and physiologic factors influence airway difficulty, including limited mouth opening, short thyromental distance, obesity, prior head and neck cancer surgery or radiation, severe hypoxia, shock, metabolic derangements, or right ventricular dysfunction. High-risk populations,&#x000a0;eg, pregnant patients, trauma patients with cervical spine injury, and morbidly obese individuals, require careful planning and anticipation. The use of video laryngoscopy, flexible fiberoptic scopes, supraglottic airways, and emergency front-of-neck access, guided by established algorithms from the DAS, ASA, and CAFG, improves first-pass success, reduces the need for repeated attempts, and minimizes complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-133971.r1">[1]</xref> Early recognition of airway predictors and physiologic derangements informs timing, technique, and device selection.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration enhances patient-centered outcomes and safety by integrating the expertise of physicians, advanced practitioners, nurses, pharmacists, and ancillary staff. Physicians lead airway assessment, procedural planning, and execution while consulting anesthesiology or surgical colleagues when complex airway management is anticipated. Nurses support preparation, monitoring, and rapid response, ensuring all equipment is available and functioning, while pharmacists assist in selecting and dosing medications to optimize hemodynamics and sedation. Respiratory therapists provide oxygenation strategies and ventilatory support. Effective communication and shared decision-making within the team, including coordination with primary care or obstetric clinicians, ensures timely referral, risk mitigation, and adherence to protocols. Documentation of airway findings and post-procedure follow-up allows continuity of care and informs future airway management, reducing the likelihood of preventable complications.</p>
      </sec>
